Defer in a loop body fires once per iteration
=============================================

A `defer stmt` inside a loop body queues a cleanup on every
iteration, not once for the whole loop. All N queued bodies
must run at function exit in LIFO order (most-recently-queued
fires first).

For a long time every codegen backend (llvm-host, svm-host,
trisc, riscv64, riscv32, wasm32) fired the defer only **once**
regardless of iteration count — the lexical-cleanup pattern
emits one cleanup site per textual `defer` source location.
Only the interpreter queued correctly. The fix is the
per-defer-site counter scheme: each `defer` source location
owns one i64 counter alloca; the defer-statement codegen emits
`counter++`; the fn-exit codegen replays the body inside
`while counter > 0`, so an N-iteration loop bumps the counter
to N and the body fires N times on exit.

See `feedback_sysl_codegen_defer_in_loop_queue_depth.md`.

**Known limitation** (intentional, pragmatic): a defer body
inside a loop cannot capture the iteration variable, because
the bodies are all replayed at fn exit after the loop has
ended. Workaround: copy the value into a struct or call a
helper that takes it by value. The counter scheme cannot model
per-iteration captures without per-defer-execution closure
environments.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

    struct DilCounter
        n: int

Loop fires N defers
-------------------

A `for` loop runs 3 iterations; each iteration queues one
`defer c.n = c.n + 1`. At fn exit all 3 fire and the counter
ends at 3.

    dil_count_increments(c: *DilCounter) -> unit
        for i in 0..<3
            defer c.n = c.n + 1

    #test
    test_defer_in_loop_fires_per_iteration() -> unit
        var c = DilCounter(0)
        dil_count_increments(&c)
        assert(c.n == 3, s"expected 3 defers, got ${c.n}")

Zero-iteration loop fires no defers
-----------------------------------

If the loop body never runs, the defer is never queued, so
nothing fires at exit. This pins that the per-iteration
queueing is dynamic, not lexical.

    dil_zero_iterations(c: *DilCounter) -> unit
        for i in 0..<0
            defer c.n = c.n + 1

    #test
    test_zero_iteration_loop_fires_no_defers() -> unit
        var c = DilCounter(0)
        dil_zero_iterations(&c)
        assert(c.n == 0, s"expected 0 defers, got ${c.n}")

While loop with variable count
------------------------------

Same idea but driven by a `while`. Confirms the per-iteration
counter mechanism doesn't depend on `for`-range desugaring.

    dil_while_count(c: *DilCounter, n: int) -> unit
        var i = 0
        while i < n
            defer c.n = c.n + 1
            i = i + 1

    #test
    test_while_loop_fires_per_iteration() -> unit
        var c = DilCounter(0)
        dil_while_count(&c, 5)
        assert(c.n == 5, s"expected 5 defers, got ${c.n}")

Two defer sites in a loop body — both counted
---------------------------------------------

Two distinct `defer` statements in one loop body — each owns
its own counter and each is queued per iteration. The exact
firing order across sites within a single iteration is
intentionally **not** pinned: the interpreter interleaves
Go-style (per-iteration LIFO), but the codegen scheme groups
by site (all instances of one site, then the other). The
*total count* — every queued body fires — is what this test
locks down. Both orderings end with `c.n` increased by 2×N.

    dil_two_sites_count_total(c: *DilCounter) -> unit
        for i in 0..<3
            defer c.n = c.n + 1
            defer c.n = c.n + 10

    #test
    test_two_defer_sites_total_count() -> unit
        var c = DilCounter(0)
        dil_two_sites_count_total(&c)
        // 3 iterations × (one +1 + one +10) = 33. Order across sites
        // diverges between backends but the sum doesn't.
        assert(c.n == 33, s"expected 33 (3×(1+10)), got ${c.n}")
